      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\), we start by expressing both sides of the equation with the same base. However, since 7 and 8 are different bases, we will take the common logarithm (log base 10) of both sides to simplify the equation. This gives us:\n\n\\[\n\\log(7^{x+7}) = \\log(8^x)\n\\]\n\nUsing the logarithm power rule \\(\\log(a^b) = b \\log(a)\\), we can rewrite the equation as:\n\n\\[\n(x+7) \\log 7 = x \\log 8\n\\]\n\nNext, we distribute the logarithms on both sides:\n\n\\[\nx \\log 7 + 7 \\log 7 = x \\log 8\n\\]\n\nNow, we can isolate the terms involving \\(x\\) on one side of the equation. Subtract \\(x \\log 7\\) from both sides:\n\n\\[\n7 \\log 7 = x \\log 8 - x \\log 7\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\(x\\) on the right side:\n\n\\[\n7 \\log 7 = x (\\log 8 - \\log 7)\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\(x\\), divide both sides by \\(\\log 8 - \\log 7\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{7 \\log 7}{\\log 8 - \\log 7}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that the solution can be expressed in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\). Therefore, we need to express \\(\\frac{7 \\log 7}{\\log 8 - \\log 7}\\) in the form \\(\\log_b 7^7\\). Using the property of logarithms \\(\\log_b (a^c) = c \\log_b a\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\log_b 7^7 = 7 \\log_b 7\n\\]\n\nBy comparing \\(\\frac{7 \\log 7}{\\log 8 - \\log 7}\\) with \\(7 \\log_b 7\\), we see that:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{7 \\log 7}{\\log 8 - \\log 7} = 7 \\log_b 7\n\\]\n\nThis implies that:\n\n\\[\n\\log_b 7 = \\frac{1}{7}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, \\(b\\) must be the base that satisfies this equation. Since the logarithm base is typically assumed to be the same as the base of the logarithm expression, we conclude that:\n\n\\[\nb = 7\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(b\\) is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).",
