      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\), we start by taking the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n\\[\n\\ln(7^{x+7}) = \\ln(8^x)\n\\]\n\nUsing the logarithm power rule \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\), we can rewrite the equation as:\n\n\\[\n(x+7) \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nNext, we expand and rearrange the equation to isolate \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 + 7 \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 - x \\ln 8 = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\(x\\) on the left side:\n\n\\[\nx (\\ln 7 - \\ln 8) = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify the fraction by recognizing that \\(\\ln 7 - \\ln 8 = \\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}\n\\]\n\nTo express \\(x\\) in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\), we need to rewrite the fraction using the properties of logarithms. Recall that \\(\\log_b a = \\frac{\\ln a}{\\ln b}\\), so we can write:\n\n\\[\nx = \\log_{\\frac{7}{8}} 7^7\n\\]\n\nFor the argument of the logarithm to match our form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\), we need to express \\(\\frac{7}{8}\\) as a base. We can rewrite \\(\\frac{7}{8}\\) as \\(8^{-1} \\cdot 7^1 = 7 \\cdot 8^{-1}\\), but it's more straightforward to use the change of base formula directly. We need to recognize that:\n\n\\[\nx = \\log_{\\frac{7}{8}} 7^7 = \\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)} = \\log_{\\frac{7}{8}} 7^7\n\\]\n\nThis means that the base \\(b\\) in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\) is \\(\\frac{7}{8}\\). Therefore, the value of \\(b\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{8}}\n\\]",
