      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\), we start by taking the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n\\[\n\\ln(7^{x+7}) = \\ln(8^x)\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of logarithms that \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\), we can rewrite the equation as:\n\n\\[\n(x+7) \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nNext, we expand and rearrange the equation to isolate \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 + 7 \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 - x \\ln 8 = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\(x\\) on the left side:\n\n\\[\nx (\\ln 7 - \\ln 8) = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8}\n\\]\n\nWe can express \\(\\ln 8\\) as \\(\\ln(2^3) = 3 \\ln 2\\), so the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - 3 \\ln 2}\n\\]\n\nWe recognize that \\(\\ln 7 - 3 \\ln 2\\) can be written as \\(\\ln 7 - \\ln 8\\), and using the property of logarithms again, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\ln 7 - \\ln 8 = \\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)\n\\]\n\nThus, the equation for \\(x\\) is:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}\n\\]\n\nWe need to express \\(x\\) in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\). Using the change of base formula for logarithms, we know that if \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\), then \\(b^x = 7^7\\). Therefore, we need to find \\(b\\) such that:\n\n\\[\nb^{\\log_b 7^7} = 7^7\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n7^7 = 7^7\n\\]\n\nThis is true, so we need to match the exponents. Since \\(b^{\\log_b 7^7} = 7^7\\), it follows that \\(b = 7\\). Therefore, the value of \\(b\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]",
